02:13:10,650 --> 02:13:13,010
Oh, you've got a great window here, huh?

2463
02:13:18,440 --> 02:13:20,140
Can I turn off the lights inside?

2464
02:13:22,220 --> 02:13:24,360
Oh, that's so cozy, look at that!

2465
02:13:25,680 --> 02:13:27,180
Turn the lights on everywhere!

2466
02:13:30,060 --> 02:13:31,800
Yeah, oh, look at them!

2467
02:13:36,560 --> 02:13:38,620
This is freaking cool, man!

2468
02:13:41,400 --> 02:13:42,420
I love it.

2469
02:13:43,660 --> 02:13:46,200
And check it out, we're gonna actually disengage this now.

2470
02:13:50,640 --> 02:13:51,620
Bye-bye.

2471
02:13:52,380 --> 02:13:54,260
Now we have a docking port!

2472
02:13:56,360 --> 02:13:57,860
We can dock later.

2473
02:14:02,720 --> 02:14:04,460
Yo, this is so cool.

2474
02:14:07,740 --> 02:14:10,980
Retract, no, let me do toggle overlay.

2475
02:14:11,980 --> 02:14:16,640
And then let's try the... was it curb net?

2476
02:14:16,800 --> 02:14:17,920
Curb net access here.

2477
02:14:20,000 --> 02:14:21,040
Nina's notch?

2478
02:14:22,320 --> 02:14:23,000
Hmm.

2479
02:14:23,920 --> 02:14:24,680
Who's Nina?

2480
02:14:26,280 --> 02:14:27,880
And what's up with her notch?

2481
02:14:35,310 --> 02:14:37,390
Let me actually fast forward slightly here.

2482
02:14:40,390 --> 02:14:42,170
Wait, my rotation stopped.

2483
02:14:44,030 --> 02:14:44,710
What?

2484
02:14:45,030 --> 02:14:46,930
Rotation stops when you fast forward.

2485
02:14:47,810 --> 02:14:48,750
The hell?

2486
02:15:02,970 --> 02:15:05,050
At some point I did this and there was a question mark.

2487
02:15:10,910 --> 02:15:13,730
But that may have been on... Minmus.

2488
02:15:13,850 --> 02:15:14,970
I forgot where I did this.

2489
02:15:16,750 --> 02:15:18,250
No, actually that was on the moon, I believe.

2490
02:15:23,330 --> 02:15:27,810
And hey, it's kind of nice to have a satellite now, technically, in polar orbit.

2491
02:15:27,970 --> 02:15:30,750
So, you know, connections are easier to have.

2492
02:15:31,670 --> 02:15:34,170
Now that I have one that is equatorial and one that is polar.

2493
02:15:37,950 --> 02:15:38,510
That's neat.

2494
02:15:40,630 --> 02:15:42,090
Oh, hold on, let me actually try something.

2495
02:15:43,550 --> 02:15:47,370
Camera... orbital... chase...

2496
02:15:51,100 --> 02:15:52,280
There we go, I like that.

2497
02:15:58,480 --> 02:15:59,840
Show me some goodies here.

2498
02:16:00,420 --> 02:16:02,480
And I'm not talking about Nina's notch.

2499
02:16:10,960 --> 02:16:12,340
Show me question marks.

2500
02:16:26,760 --> 02:16:27,880
Maybe I need to do this.

2501
02:16:28,940 --> 02:16:31,040
Oh no, definitely not like that.

2502
02:16:35,000 --> 02:16:36,560
Does it matter where I have this thing?

2503
02:16:39,320 --> 02:16:40,000
Probably not.

2504
02:16:49,360 --> 02:16:49,940
Whoa.

2505
02:16:50,240 --> 02:16:51,320
Oh, it's dark now.

2506
02:16:52,160 --> 02:16:53,280
But we have lights on.

2507
02:16:54,820 --> 02:16:55,800
So cozy.

2508
02:16:58,140 --> 02:16:59,360
Yo, look at this up here.

2509
02:17:01,640 --> 02:17:05,020
It's like a massive streak of... something.

2510
02:17:05,920 --> 02:17:06,500
Channels?

2511
02:17:07,260 --> 02:17:12,240
I remember people went crazy about the potential of channels on Mars, you know?

2512
02:17:12,860 --> 02:17:15,140
There's alien civilizations on Mars!

2513
02:17:15,400 --> 02:17:16,080
There's proof, look!

2514
02:17:16,510 --> 02:17:18,470
They're transporting liquid from the poles.

2515
02:17:19,490 --> 02:17:20,610
It's real, people.

2516
02:17:23,610 --> 02:17:24,610
If only, huh?

2517
02:17:24,870 --> 02:17:25,310
If only.

2518
02:17:28,770 --> 02:17:30,190
God, that looks so good.

2519
02:17:30,270 --> 02:17:30,850
Oh, my God.

2520
02:17:31,030 --> 02:17:35,910
Let me... I mean... Fucking hell.

2521
02:17:37,410 --> 02:17:41,390
Yo, the terrain looks rugged on the poles.

2522
02:17:41,570 --> 02:17:42,330
Look at that down there.

2523
02:17:47,630 --> 02:17:48,390
Dang, son.

2524
02:17:58,330 --> 02:17:59,430
I... You know what?

2525
02:17:59,490 --> 02:18:00,590
This is like half the fun for me.

2526
02:18:00,670 --> 02:18:02,470
Just having these views and just looking at it.

2527
02:18:09,170 --> 02:18:10,430
How can you not love this?

2528
02:18:14,830 --> 02:18:15,770
So amazing.

2529
02:18:15,950 --> 02:18:17,330
I mean, look at the terrain down there.

2530
02:18:18,330 --> 02:18:22,250
It is, like... really messed up.

2531
02:18:25,510 --> 02:18:28,510
Looks like the moon got beat really hard down there.

2532
02:18:30,030 --> 02:18:31,570
Okay, let me just keep refreshing.

2533
02:18:32,650 --> 02:18:35,330
It's not showing the frickin' question marks, though.

2534
02:18:43,610 --> 02:18:44,650
Huge crater up there.

2535
02:18:45,650 --> 02:18:47,630
And two craters right next to each other.

2536
02:18:49,590 --> 02:18:50,530
Twin craters.

2537
02:18:54,070 --> 02:18:54,470
Wait.

2538
02:18:55,010 --> 02:18:56,430
Triple craters.

2539
02:18:56,770 --> 02:18:57,990
You know, it actually makes sense.

2540
02:18:59,670 --> 02:19:12,570
Maybe not with the moon, but... A lot of the time when, you know, asteroids come in, or meteors, for example, on, you know, approaching Jupiter, tidal forces will break it apart, you know, and it will produce bang-bang-bang-bang-bang impacts along a line.

2541
02:19:15,890 --> 02:19:17,950
That's probably not what's happening here, but...

2542
02:19:22,000 --> 02:19:23,540
I think it was called Shoemaker-Levy.

2543
02:19:23,760 --> 02:19:28,300
It was one of those asteroids that crashed into Jupiter.

2544
02:19:28,420 --> 02:19:29,560
And we all had observations of it.

2545
02:19:29,620 --> 02:19:31,520
And we could see it go boom, boom, boom on the surface, you know?

2546
02:19:32,320 --> 02:19:34,040
You could see the explosions in real time.

2547
02:19:34,040 --> 02:19:34,780
We followed it.

2548
02:19:35,060 --> 02:19:35,860
It's awesome.

2549
02:19:40,260 --> 02:19:40,620
Okay.

2550
02:19:41,720 --> 02:19:42,900
Mission successful, baby.

2551
02:19:43,200 --> 02:19:44,240
I call that a W.

2552
02:19:45,160 --> 02:19:46,060
Back to Space Center.

2553
02:19:51,790 --> 02:19:52,510
Next day.

2554
02:19:55,780 --> 02:19:56,220
Alrighty.

2555
02:19:57,020 --> 02:19:57,320
Oh, man.

2556
02:19:57,380 --> 02:19:58,580
We're running so low on signs here.

2557
02:19:58,720 --> 02:20:00,760
But we have the lab now doing things.

2558
02:20:01,040 --> 02:20:03,820
So hopefully very soon we're gonna get some signs out of that.

2559
02:20:05,100 --> 02:20:05,540
Okay.

2560
02:20:05,680 --> 02:20:06,360
Explore Eve.

2561
02:20:06,700 --> 02:20:07,140
Right.

2562
02:20:07,220 --> 02:20:09,100
That's the one we have had for a long time.

2563
02:20:10,700 --> 02:20:14,780
I think, guys, that it is time that we go to Duna.

2564
02:20:15,980 --> 02:20:16,580
That's right.

2565
02:20:17,200 --> 02:20:18,820
We're gonna make a Duna lander.

2566
02:20:20,660 --> 02:20:21,100
Duna.

2567
02:20:21,340 --> 02:20:21,640
Duna.

2568
02:20:22,060 --> 02:20:23,320
And we're gonna fast forward time.

2569
02:20:23,500 --> 02:20:24,420
We're gonna simply wait.

2570
02:20:25,040 --> 02:20:29,040
Because, you know... We gotta wait for the windows to open.

2571
02:20:29,240 --> 02:20:31,560
It's just the reality of space flight, you know?

2572
02:20:32,060 --> 02:20:35,060
Especially when you have limited money... and limited fuel.

2573
02:20:35,940 --> 02:20:37,860
Which I guess means the same thing in this world.

2574
02:20:39,100 --> 02:20:43,280
Let me grab my EVE... as a starting point.

2575
02:20:43,840 --> 02:20:44,580
There we go.

2576
02:20:44,660 --> 02:20:46,900
We're gonna call this... Duna?

2577
02:20:48,300 --> 02:20:49,720
Dude, let's make a rover!

2578
02:20:51,300 --> 02:20:54,680
If we go to Mars, I mean, we gotta have a rover, right?

2579
02:20:55,880 --> 02:20:56,680
Duna rover.

2580
02:20:58,560 --> 02:21:05,520
It'll be... It'll be rolling on Mars.

2581
02:21:05,860 --> 02:21:06,440
Or Duna.

2582
02:21:08,280 --> 02:21:08,720
Yeah.

2583
02:21:09,180 --> 02:21:09,380
Good.

2584
02:21:09,680 --> 02:21:09,900
Good.

2585
02:21:10,440 --> 02:21:12,220
Now, this design is tried and true, of course.

2586
02:21:12,600 --> 02:21:14,080
Not gonna mess with it too much.

2587
02:21:14,600 --> 02:21:16,540
But up here, we are gonna change a couple things.

2588
02:21:17,420 --> 02:21:18,420
What am I gonna change?

2589
02:21:19,740 --> 02:21:20,260
Let's see.

2590
02:21:20,600 --> 02:21:23,720
We can get rid of this first so we can get some better views here.

2591
02:21:27,620 --> 02:21:29,120
Duna has an atmosphere, right?

2592
02:21:29,480 --> 02:21:34,940
If we're doing Mars equivalent, I feel like it should work with a parachute, but I don't know.

2593
02:21:36,140 --> 02:21:37,080
Maybe it's not enough.

2594
02:21:37,200 --> 02:21:41,180
Maybe the atmosphere is so thin that it's simply not going to be enough.

2595
02:21:41,320 --> 02:21:45,300
So having some thrusters, just in case, could be nice.

2596
02:21:46,640 --> 02:21:48,240
Obviously we're sending a probe.

2597
02:21:52,380 --> 02:21:55,180
What would be a good design, though?

2598
02:21:56,270 --> 02:21:57,350
Maybe we use this?

2599
02:21:58,350 --> 02:21:59,770
No, that's for people, isn't it?

2600
02:22:02,210 --> 02:22:03,450
Dude, why don't we send people?

2601
02:22:03,530 --> 02:22:04,050
Should we send people?

2602
02:22:04,550 --> 02:22:05,770
I feel like we should send people.

2603
02:22:07,890 --> 02:22:10,090
Nah, we gotta learn first.

2604
02:22:10,090 --> 02:22:10,910
We have to learn.

2605
02:22:11,210 --> 02:22:14,690
And not only that, I'm gonna take measurements of the atmosphere.

2606
02:22:14,930 --> 02:22:17,430
Because we now have an atmosphere measurement device.

2607
02:22:19,230 --> 02:22:19,970
Where is it?

2608
02:22:21,490 --> 02:22:22,190
That one.

2609
02:22:22,890 --> 02:22:23,790
No, wait, not that one.

2610
02:22:24,610 --> 02:22:25,670
It's this one.

2611
02:22:26,230 --> 02:22:28,150
Atmospheric fluid spectrovariometer.

2612
02:22:28,830 --> 02:22:31,090
I'm gonna be measuring that atmosphere so hard.

2613
02:22:31,730 --> 02:22:32,810
It's gonna be great.

2614
02:22:34,550 --> 02:22:35,370
What's this?

2615
02:22:36,670 --> 02:22:37,930
Oh yeah, the probe, okay.

2616
02:22:38,630 --> 02:22:39,570
We actually have two probes.

2617
02:22:40,590 --> 02:22:40,830
Yeah.

2618
02:22:42,030 --> 02:22:44,390
I mean, this design is pretty good.

2619
02:22:44,910 --> 02:22:45,950
You know what?

2620
02:22:50,320 --> 02:22:51,460
No, we're not gonna make a rover.

2621
02:22:52,420 --> 02:22:57,600
We are, however, gonna... not have this part up here.

2622
02:22:59,380 --> 02:23:00,720
Then again, it is a good part.

2623
02:23:00,800 --> 02:23:01,340
This is a good part.

2624
02:23:03,220 --> 02:23:04,940
Oh, yeah, we need a satellite in orbit.

2625
02:23:05,120 --> 02:23:05,980
Oh, I forgot about that.

2626
02:23:06,100 --> 02:23:09,940
So we need actually... I was just like to say we land with this, but no, we can't do that.

2627
02:23:11,680 --> 02:23:12,860
We can't do it, Captain.

2628
02:23:14,800 --> 02:23:17,240
Uh... Let me think.

2629
02:23:17,740 --> 02:23:19,100
I think I'll keep the heat shield.

2630
02:23:21,400 --> 02:23:22,260
I mean, okay.

2631
02:23:24,000 --> 02:23:25,340
We don't know what we're dealing with.

2632
02:23:25,400 --> 02:23:26,440
Let's just send this, shall we?

2633
02:23:27,000 --> 02:23:28,020
I'm gonna add this guy.

2634
02:23:28,180 --> 02:23:29,300
Oh my god, it's massive.

2635
02:23:30,380 --> 02:23:30,780
Frick.

2636
02:23:32,220 --> 02:23:32,820
Oh no.

2637
02:23:33,660 --> 02:23:39,740
Maybe we add... I don't know, maybe a little more... service bay?

2638
02:23:40,720 --> 02:23:41,120
Perhaps.

2639
02:23:42,820 --> 02:23:43,340
Rotate it.

2640
02:23:47,640 --> 02:23:50,240
No, we would have to do it like that, I guess, and then we could put this guy like that.

2641
02:23:51,260 --> 02:23:56,340
Ooh, that's gonna make it... not good, not balanced.

2642
02:23:58,800 --> 02:23:59,960
I'm afraid, okay?

2643
02:24:00,480 --> 02:24:01,560
This scares me.

2644
02:24:03,920 --> 02:24:05,780
Let me see the weight distribution here.

2645
02:24:09,260 --> 02:24:10,740
I mean, okay, that doesn't look bad at all.

2646
02:24:15,560 --> 02:24:16,760
This barely moves it.

2647
02:24:16,920 --> 02:24:17,180
What the?

2648
02:24:18,300 --> 02:24:18,880
Hell?

2649
02:24:20,000 --> 02:24:21,120
How much does that weigh?

2650
02:24:22,060 --> 02:24:22,640
What?!

2651
02:24:22,640 --> 02:24:26,400
It weighs as much as this bullshit.

2652
02:24:26,980 --> 02:24:28,380
That has to be a bug.

2653
02:24:30,740 --> 02:24:31,880
That's not intended.

2654
02:24:33,100 --> 02:24:35,300
This has to weigh more.

2655
02:24:37,340 --> 02:24:38,640
No way.

2656
02:24:41,240 --> 02:24:42,400
I don't believe it.

2657
02:24:46,870 --> 02:24:47,910
Let's bring this guy.

2658
02:24:48,190 --> 02:24:49,410
Oh, we already have it.

2659
02:24:49,530 --> 02:24:49,590
Okay.

2660
02:24:50,970 --> 02:24:52,230
What about the surface stuff?

2661
02:24:52,970 --> 02:24:53,550
We have it already.

2662
02:24:53,710 --> 02:24:53,890
Damn.

2663
02:24:57,360 --> 02:24:59,580
I just want to put something over here to offset it, you know?

2664
02:25:00,900 --> 02:25:03,400
Let's not forget, we also have this real estate down there.

2665
02:25:03,480 --> 02:25:04,040
Oh, wait a second.

2666
02:25:05,820 --> 02:25:06,560
I'm a genius.

2667
02:25:09,380 --> 02:25:11,660
No, you're not gonna... What do you mean?

2668
02:25:15,980 --> 02:25:17,080
Come on, man.

2669
02:25:17,600 --> 02:25:17,920
Really?

2670
02:25:18,040 --> 02:25:18,920
Why won't you do that?

2671
02:25:19,500 --> 02:25:20,180
Okay, that works.

2672
02:25:22,500 --> 02:25:23,340
Slide it in.

2673
02:25:26,920 --> 02:25:28,560
It's not going to measure shit in there, is it?

2674
02:25:29,500 --> 02:25:32,840
It needs to measure... atmosphere.

2675
02:25:35,140 --> 02:25:36,520
Yeah, look at this intake up here.

2676
02:25:37,500 --> 02:25:37,780
Yep.

2677
02:25:38,300 --> 02:25:39,460
So we actually want to turn it around.

2678
02:25:41,040 --> 02:25:43,360
Not that I think it matters, but, you know, it matters to me.

2679
02:25:46,680 --> 02:25:47,160
There we go.

2680
02:25:47,220 --> 02:25:51,480
So when we fall down, you know, it's going to suck up all that atmosphere, and we're going to get some sweet data.

2681
02:25:57,600 --> 02:25:58,360
I like it.

2682
02:26:01,740 --> 02:26:04,720
Although, ideally we put all of... Actually, yeah.

2683
02:26:06,800 --> 02:26:07,280
No!

2684
02:26:08,800 --> 02:26:09,280
Okay.

2685
02:26:11,040 --> 02:26:11,820
Wait, wait, wait.

2686
02:26:12,220 --> 02:26:13,240
Let me put this guy on top.

2687
02:26:17,620 --> 02:26:18,960
Why are you like this?

2688
02:26:21,860 --> 02:26:22,300
Yes.

2689
02:26:23,060 --> 02:26:23,500
Yes.

2690
02:26:23,840 --> 02:26:24,760
All the mass at the bottom.

2691
02:26:27,400 --> 02:26:27,840
Incredible.

2692
02:26:29,140 --> 02:26:30,180
That's what I'm talking about.

2693
02:26:31,980 --> 02:26:33,640
That's gonna make it so much more stable, too.

2694
02:26:34,420 --> 02:26:35,120
Very nice.

2695
02:26:35,660 --> 02:26:40,980
This thing... I mean, if it's made for atmosphere, surely it's gonna, you know, survive a lot.

2696
02:26:44,480 --> 02:26:44,960
Tolerance.

2697
02:26:46,080 --> 02:26:46,960
1200k, wait.

2698
02:26:48,920 --> 02:26:49,820
Is that enough?

2699
02:26:52,880 --> 02:26:53,360
Ew.

2700
02:26:54,400 --> 02:26:55,120
Wait a minute.

2701
02:26:56,560 --> 02:26:57,880
That's not gonna be enough, is it?

2702
02:27:03,140 --> 02:27:06,560
Maybe it's meant to be used when you're, like, slowly descending anyway?

2703
02:27:08,880 --> 02:27:09,360
Fuck.

2704
02:27:10,020 --> 02:27:11,840
I guess I put this guy inside anyway.

2705
02:27:15,420 --> 02:27:17,740
I mean, we can open these doors, right, and then it'll be fine.

2706
02:27:18,960 --> 02:27:21,060
Let's not, you know, overthink this too much.

2707
02:27:22,960 --> 02:27:25,080
Yeah, it'll be, like, yeah, it's fine.

2708
02:27:25,640 --> 02:27:26,560
Don't worry about it.

2709
02:27:30,380 --> 02:27:32,380
We just gotta protect it, you know, against the elements.

2710
02:27:32,920 --> 02:27:34,560
And it doesn't weigh shit, so it's fine.

2711
02:27:35,500 --> 02:27:40,260
Oh, it's not actually... centered.

2712
02:27:40,680 --> 02:27:41,360
There we go.

2713
02:27:43,930 --> 02:27:46,410
And, let me see, it's not quite centered here either.

2714
02:27:50,350 --> 02:27:51,850
I would say that's pretty good.

2715
02:27:51,990 --> 02:27:53,510
Maybe a little in like that.

2716
02:27:55,470 --> 02:27:56,070
Yeah.

2717
02:27:56,690 --> 02:27:57,650
Close it up.

2718
02:27:58,470 --> 02:27:59,070
Alright.

2719
02:27:59,830 --> 02:28:00,810
Anything else?

2720
02:28:01,990 --> 02:28:03,550
Surface scanning module.

2721
02:28:05,230 --> 02:28:06,910
Ooh, that's big.

2722
02:28:08,770 --> 02:28:10,230
Somehow it weighs the same.

2723
02:28:15,520 --> 02:28:16,260
Oh my god.

2724
02:28:18,000 --> 02:28:19,500
Wait, why is it so big?

